academic.explanation:
Numerical methods provide approximate solutions to mathematical problems where analytical closed-form solutions are impossible or intractable.
1. Error Analysis:
   • Absolute Error: `E_a = |True Value - Approximate Value| = |x - x_approx|`;
   • Relative Error: `E_r = E_a / |True Value| = |x - x_approx| / |x|`;
   • Percentage Error: `E_p = E_r * 100%`.
2. Bisection Method (Bolzano's Intermediate Value Theorem):
   If a continuous function `f(x)` satisfies `f(a) * f(b) < 0`, there exists at least one real root between `a` and `b`.
   • Algorithm Steps:
     1. Set `x_mid = (a + b) / 2`;
     2. Evaluate `f(x_mid)`;
     3. If `f(x_mid) == 0`, root found;
     4. If `f(a) * f(x_mid) < 0`, root lies in `[a, x_mid]`; set `b = x_mid`;
     5. Else, root lies in `[x_mid, b]`; set `a = x_mid`;
     6. Repeat until `|b - a| < tolerance`.
   • Convergence: The interval halves every iteration: `|e_{n+1}| = 0.5 * |e_n|`. It has a linear rate of convergence (Order 1). It is guaranteed to converge, but convergence is slow.

